\section{Failed replays}{

A replay fails on a string when one of its steps cannot be carried out:
an \code{encode} step meets a character its codec does not have, or a
\code{decode} step meets bytes its codec cannot read. Nothing is guessed or
skipped: the call is an error of class \code{ftfy_replay_failed}, which names
the first element that failed, the step and the reason, and no text is
returned.

A plan can also be impossible on every string, because its steps do not
fit together. An \code{encode} step turns text into bytes and a \code{decode}
step turns bytes into text; the fixers \code{restore_byte_a0} and
\code{replace_lossy_sequences} work on bytes, and every other fixer and
\code{normalize} work on text. A plan that decodes text, encodes bytes, hands
a fixer the wrong one of the two or ends with bytes fails on every
string that is not missing.

A name that is not an accepted action, codec, fixer or normalisation
form is an error before anything is replayed. See \code{\link[=ftfy_plan]{ftfy_plan()}} for the
accepted names.
}

